INSERT INTO topic_sections (topic_id, title, content, section_type, order_index) VALUES
('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000002',
'Concept Overview: Circles and Their Properties',
E'## Circle Geometry: The Big Picture\n\nA circle is defined as the set of all points in a plane that are at a fixed distance (the radius) from a fixed point (the centre). This simple definition generates a rich collection of geometric theorems that govern the relationships between angles, chords, tangents, and arcs.\n\n**Circle geometry is visual, elegant, and highly predictable.** Once you know the six key theorems and the tangent properties, you can solve virtually any circle geometry problem.\n\n**Topics covered in this chapter:**\n- The complete vocabulary of circle geometry\n- The six fundamental circle theorems (with proofs)\n- Tangent properties (perpendicularity and equal lengths)\n- Intersecting chord theorem\n- Cyclic quadrilateral properties\n- Applying multiple theorems in compound problems\n\n**WASSCE Exam Significance:**\nCircle theorems appear in every single WASSCE Mathematics paper without exception. Typically 2-3 questions (worth 8-12 marks in theory). Students who can identify which theorem applies and state it clearly earn full marks. Students who just guess angle values earn nothing.\n\n**Key Skill:** In WASSCE, you must not only find the angle — you must **state the theorem** that justifies each step. Practice writing: "Angle at centre = 2 × angle at circumference" as a reason.',
'overview', 1),

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000002',
'Definitions: All Parts of a Circle',
E'## Complete Circle Vocabulary\n\n### The Fundamental Parts\n\n**Centre (O)**: The fixed point equidistant from every point on the circle. All radii are equal.\n\n**Radius (r)**: The distance from the centre to any point on the circumference. Plural: radii.\n\n**Diameter (d)**: A chord that passes through the centre. The longest possible chord. `d = 2r`\n\n**Circumference**: The perimeter (total boundary length) of a circle. `C = 2πr = πd`\n\n**Chord**: A straight line segment connecting any two points on the circumference. The diameter is the longest chord.\n\n**Arc**: A portion of the circumference between two points.\n- **Minor arc**: The smaller arc between two points\n- **Major arc**: The larger arc between two points\n- **Semicircle**: Half the circumference\n\n### Regions\n\n**Sector**: A "pie slice" — the region enclosed by two radii and an arc.\n- **Minor sector**: The smaller sector\n- **Major sector**: The larger sector\n\n**Segment**: The region between a chord and the arc it cuts off.\n- **Minor segment**: The smaller region (not containing the centre)\n- **Major segment**: The larger region (containing the centre)\n\n**Semicircle**: Half a circle — a diameter creates two equal semicircles.\n\n### Lines and Angles\n\n**Tangent**: A straight line that touches the circle at exactly ONE point.\n- The point of contact is called the **point of tangency**.\n- A tangent is ALWAYS perpendicular to the radius at the point of tangency.\n\n**Normal**: A line perpendicular to the tangent at the point of tangency (same as the radius extended).\n\n**Angle at the Centre**: An angle formed at the centre O by two radii. Also called **central angle**.\n\n**Angle at the Circumference**: An angle formed at a point on the circumference by two chords. Also called **inscribed angle**.\n\n**Subtended Angle**: An angle is "subtended" by an arc or chord — the arc/chord is directly opposite the angle.\n\n### Special Quadrilaterals\n\n**Cyclic Quadrilateral**: A quadrilateral whose four vertices all lie on a single circle.\n- Also called an "inscribed quadrilateral."\n- Its opposite angles are supplementary (add to 180°).\n\n**Cyclic Polygon**: Any polygon whose vertices all lie on a circle.',
'definitions', 2),

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000002',
'The Six Circle Theorems — Deep Explanation',
E'## The Six Theorems You Must Absolutely Know\n\n---\n\n### THEOREM 1: Angle at Centre is Twice Angle at Circumference\n\n**Statement**: The angle subtended by an arc (or chord) at the centre of a circle is TWICE the angle subtended by the same arc at any point on the remaining part of the circumference.\n\n`Angle AOB = 2 × Angle ACB`\n(where O is centre, A and B are on the circle, C is on the major arc)\n\n**Why it works**: Triangle OAC is isosceles (OA = OC = radius). So angles OCA = OAC. Similarly triangle OBC is isosceles. When you work out the exterior angles, you get the central angle = twice the circumferential angle.\n\n**In WASSCE, write**: "Angle at centre = twice angle at circumference (same arc)"\n\n**Key application**: If told the centre angle, halve it to get the circumference angle. If told the circumference angle, double it to get the centre angle.\n\n---\n\n### THEOREM 2: Angles in the Same Segment are Equal\n\n**Statement**: Angles subtended by the same chord at the same side of the circle are equal.\n\nIf P and Q are both on the SAME arc (same side of chord AB), then:\n`Angle APB = Angle AQB`\n\n**Why it works**: Both angles are "half" of the same central angle (by Theorem 1), so they must be equal to each other.\n\n**In WASSCE, write**: "Angles in the same segment are equal"\n\n---\n\n### THEOREM 3: Angle in a Semicircle is 90°\n\n**Statement**: An angle inscribed in a semicircle (i.e., the angle subtended by a diameter at the circumference) is always a right angle (90°).\n\nIf AB is a diameter and C is any point on the circle:\n`Angle ACB = 90°` (always)\n\n**Why it works**: This is a special case of Theorem 1. The central angle for a semicircle is 180° (a straight line). By Theorem 1: circumference angle = 180°/2 = 90°.\n\n**In WASSCE, write**: "Angle in a semicircle = 90°" or "Angle subtended by diameter at circumference = 90°"\n\nThis theorem is extremely common in WASSCE — look for a diameter and a point on the circle.\n\n---\n\n### THEOREM 4: Opposite Angles of a Cyclic Quadrilateral are Supplementary\n\n**Statement**: The opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral add up to 180°.\n\nFor cyclic quadrilateral ABCD:\n`Angle A + Angle C = 180°`\n`Angle B + Angle D = 180°`\n\n**Why it works**: Each pair of opposite angles subtends arcs that together make the full circle (360°). By Theorem 1, each angle is half its arc, so together they give 180°.\n\n**In WASSCE, write**: "Opposite angles of cyclic quadrilateral are supplementary"\n\n---\n\n### THEOREM 5: Tangent-Radius Perpendicularity\n\n**Statement**: A tangent drawn to a circle is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact.\n\nIf OT is a radius and TL is a tangent at T:\n`Angle OTL = 90°`\n\n**Why it works**: The tangent touches at exactly one point. If it were not perpendicular, a nearby point would be closer to the centre, making the tangent cross the circle — contradiction.\n\n---\n\n### THEOREM 6: Tangents from an External Point are Equal\n\n**Statement**: The two tangent segments drawn from an external point to a circle are equal in length.\n\nIf PA and PB are tangents from external point P:\n`PA = PB`\n\n**Proof**: Triangles OAP and OBP are congruent (OA = OB = radius, OP is common, angle OAP = angle OBP = 90°). Therefore PA = PB.\n\n---\n\n### BONUS: Intersecting Chords Theorem\n\nIf two chords AB and CD intersect at point P INSIDE the circle:\n`PA × PB = PC × PD`\n\nIf two secants are drawn from external point P:\n`PA × PB = PC × PD` (measuring full lengths from P)\n\n---\n\n## Perpendicular from Centre to Chord\n\nA perpendicular from the centre to a chord **bisects the chord** (cuts it exactly in half).\n\nConversely, the line from the centre to the midpoint of a chord is perpendicular to the chord.\n\nThis allows you to use Pythagoras: if radius = r, half-chord = h, distance from centre to chord = d:\n`r² = h² + d²`',
'explanation', 3),

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000002',
'Worked Examples — All Theorem Types',
E'## Example 1: Angle at Centre\n\n**O is the centre of a circle. Chord AB subtends angle AOB = 130° at the centre. Find angle ACB where C is a point on the major arc.**\n\nBy Theorem 1: Angle at centre = 2 × angle at circumference\n130° = 2 × Angle ACB\nAngle ACB = 65°\n\n**Answer: 65°**\n\n---\n\n## Example 2: Angles in Same Segment\n\n**In a circle, P and Q are two points on the same arc. Angle PAR = 38° and angle QAR = 38°. Explain why.**\n\nBoth angles PAR and QAR subtend the same chord AR from the same side.\nBy Theorem 2: Angles in the same segment are equal.\nTherefore angle PAR = angle QAR = 38°.\n\n---\n\n## Example 3: Angle in Semicircle\n\n**AB is a diameter of a circle. C is a point on the circle. If angle CAB = 35°, find angle ABC.**\n\nSince AB is a diameter:\nBy Theorem 3: Angle ACB = 90° (angle in semicircle)\n\nIn triangle ACB:\nAngle CAB + Angle ABC + Angle ACB = 180°\n35° + Angle ABC + 90° = 180°\nAngle ABC = 55°\n\n**Answer: 55°**\n\n---\n\n## Example 4: Cyclic Quadrilateral\n\n**PQRS is a cyclic quadrilateral. Angle P = 3x + 10 and angle R = 2x + 20. Find x and both angles.**\n\nOpposite angles of cyclic quadrilateral sum to 180°:\n(3x + 10) + (2x + 20) = 180°\n5x + 30 = 180°\n5x = 150°\nx = 30°\n\nAngle P = 3(30) + 10 = 100°\nAngle R = 2(30) + 20 = 80°\n\n**Check**: 100° + 80° = 180° ✓\n\n---\n\n## Example 5: Tangent from External Point\n\n**Two tangents PA and PB are drawn from external point P to a circle. PA = 4x - 3 and PB = 2x + 7. Find PA.**\n\nTangents from external point are equal:\n4x - 3 = 2x + 7\n2x = 10\nx = 5\n\nPA = 4(5) - 3 = 17\n\n**Answer: PA = 17 units**\n\n---\n\n## Example 6: Perpendicular from Centre (Pythagoras)\n\n**A chord of length 16 cm is 6 cm from the centre of a circle. Find the radius.**\n\nThe perpendicular from centre bisects the chord.\nHalf-chord = 16/2 = 8 cm\nDistance from centre = 6 cm\n\nUsing Pythagoras: r² = 8² + 6² = 64 + 36 = 100\nr = 10 cm\n\n**Answer: radius = 10 cm**\n\n---\n\n## Example 7: Intersecting Chords\n\n**Two chords PQ and RS intersect at T inside the circle. PT = 4, TQ = 9, RT = 6. Find TS.**\n\nIntersecting chords: PT × TQ = RT × TS\n4 × 9 = 6 × TS\n36 = 6 × TS\nTS = 6\n\n**Answer: TS = 6 cm**\n\n---\n\n## Example 8: Multi-Theorem Problem (WASSCE Style)\n\n**In the diagram, O is the centre of the circle. OA = OB (radii). Angle OAB = 32°. Find angle ACB where C is on the major arc.**\n\nSince OA = OB (radii), triangle OAB is isosceles.\nAngle OAB = Angle OBA = 32°\nAngle AOB = 180° - 32° - 32° = 116° (angles in triangle)\n\nBy Theorem 1: Angle ACB = Angle AOB / 2 = 116° / 2 = 58°\n\n**Answer: 58°**',
'examples', 4),

('b1000001-0000-0000-0000-000000000002',
'Common Mistakes + Practice Questions',
E'## Common Mistakes in Circle Geometry\n\n**Mistake 1**: Applying Theorem 1 the wrong way round.\n- WRONG: Central angle = half of circumference angle\n- CORRECT: **Central angle = TWICE circumference angle**\n\n**Mistake 2**: Applying Theorem 4 to a quadrilateral that is NOT inscribed in a circle.\n- Only use "opposite angles sum to 180°" for CYCLIC quadrilaterals.\n\n**Mistake 3**: Forgetting the tangent-radius angle is 90°.\n- Any time you see a tangent at a point, the radius to that point is perpendicular.\n\n**Mistake 4**: Not using the isosceles triangle property when two radii are drawn.\n- Whenever OA and OB are both radii, triangle OAB is isosceles. The base angles are equal.\n\n**Mistake 5**: Not stating the theorem used in WASSCE.\n- Examiners award marks for stating the reason. "Angle at centre = 2 × angle at circumference (same arc)" must be written.\n\n---\n\n## Practice Questions\n\n### Section A: Multiple Choice\n\n**Q1:** O is the centre. Angle AOB = 80°. Angle ACB (C on major arc) = ?\n(A) 160°   (B) 80°   (C) 40°   (D) 100°\n\n**Q2:** ABCD is a cyclic quadrilateral. Angle B = 115°. Angle D = ?\n(A) 115°   (B) 65°   (C) 245°   (D) 55°\n\n**Q3:** Which theorem states that a tangent is perpendicular to the radius?\n(A) Angle in semicircle   (B) Tangent-radius theorem   (C) Intersecting chords   (D) Cyclic quadrilateral theorem\n\n**Q4:** A chord is 24 cm long. The distance from the centre to the chord is 5 cm. Find the radius.\n(A) 10 cm   (B) 12 cm   (C) 13 cm   (D) 15 cm\n\n### Section B: Theory\n\n**Q5:** O is the centre of a circle. AB is a chord. OM is perpendicular to AB and M is the midpoint of AB. If OM = 8 cm and AB = 12 cm, find the radius of the circle.\n\n**Q6:** Two tangents TA and TB are drawn from an external point T to a circle with centre O. Prove that OT bisects angle AOB.\n\n**Q7:** In a circle, ABCD is a cyclic quadrilateral. Angle A = (2x + 15)° and angle C = (x + 30)°. Find x and state both angles.\n\n### Section C: Exam Standard\n\n**Q8:** Two chords AB and CD intersect at P inside the circle. AP = 3 cm, PB = 8 cm, and CP = 4 cm. (a) Find PD. (b) If the chords are equal, find AB.\n\n---\n\n## Solutions\n\n**Q1:** C — Angle ACB = 80°/2 = 40°\n\n**Q2:** B — Opposite angles sum to 180°: D = 180° - 115° = 65°\n\n**Q3:** B\n\n**Q4:** C — half chord = 12, distance = 5, r² = 144 + 25 = 169, r = 13\n\n**Q5:** r² = OM² + AM² = 64 + 36 = 100, r = 10 cm\n\n**Q6:** OA = OB (radii), OT is common, TA = TB (tangents from T are equal). Triangles OAT and OBT are congruent (SSS). Therefore angle AOT = angle BOT, so OT bisects angle AOB.\n\n**Q7:** (2x+15) + (x+30) = 180°; 3x + 45 = 180; x = 45°; Angle A = 105°, Angle C = 75°\n\n**Q8:** (a) AP × PB = CP × PD; 3×8 = 4×PD; PD = 6 cm. (b) AB = AP + PB = 11 cm; CD = CP + PD = 10 cm. If equal, there is a contradiction — the chords are not equal in this case.',
'practice', 5);
